  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 332 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nEarly-morning observations across northeast Kansas show a classic\nradiational cooling setup with clear skies, light winds, and cool\ntemperatures in the upper 40s to low 50s. Shallow fog and mist have \ndeveloped in some valleys and near area rivers, particularly where \nsoils remain moist from recent heavy rainfall and winds are calm. \nSurface pressure remains high, indicative of a broad surface ridge \ncentered over the central Plains.\n\nShort-term guidance support rapid erosion of any fog shortly after \nsunrise as mixing increases. Once fog clears, skies will be mostly \nsunny across the entire forecast area with only thin high clouds \npossible on the periphery of the ridge. Highs this afternoon should \nreach the low to mid 70s in most locations, running near to slightly \nbelow early October normals. Winds will remain light, generally north\nto northeast at 5 to 10 mph during the morning, veering more \neasterly or variable later in the day as the surface ridge shifts \neast. Dew points have fallen into the 40s and low 50s, so the air \nwill feel noticeably drier than recent days.\n\nTonight, clear skies, light winds, and continued dry air will again\nfavor efficient radiational cooling. Lows are expected to fall into\nthe upper 40s to low 50s area-wide, with the coolest readings in\ntraditional cold pockets of north-central Kansas and sheltered\nvalleys along the Kansas and Republican Rivers. Another round of\npatchy valley fog cannot be ruled out toward daybreak Monday, but\ncoverage should remain limited and primarily confined to low-lying\nareas.\n\nFrom Monday through Wednesday, large-scale pattern evolution features\na strengthening midlevel ridge over the central U.S. while the\nprimary storm track remains displaced to the north over the northern\nPlains and Great Lakes. At the surface, high pressure gradually\nslides east, allowing a more consistent southerly component to\ndevelop in the low-level flow across Kansas. This will support a\ngradual warming trend and slow return of moisture. High temperatures\nare expected to climb from the mid to upper 70s Monday into the upper\n70s to low 80s Tuesday and Wednesday. Skies should remain mostly \nclear to partly cloudy, and forecast guidance shows little in the way\nof organized lift or moisture convergence over the area during this \nperiod. As a result, precipitation chances remain near zero and any \ncloud cover will be tied mainly to passing high clouds or weak warm \nadvection layers.\n\nThe Thursday through Saturday period continues to show above-normal\nconfidence in a dry forecast. Ensemble and deterministic guidance\nmaintain the ridge either overhead or only slightly flattened by\nweak shortwaves passing well north of northeast Kansas. Low-level\nsoutherly flow increases somewhat, supporting highs in the low 80s\nfor many locations late in the week, with perhaps mid 80s in parts of\nnorth-central and central Kansas if mixing is efficient. Dew points\nrise into the 50s and low 60s but remain insufficient, in the absence\nof stronger forcing, to support more than isolated, low-probability\nshowers or thunderstorms along any weak boundaries. Current\nindications suggest deeper moisture and stronger ascent remain\nfocused well away from the area through at least next weekend.",
